# Vote on the Demands for Grants for 1967-68
Tamil title: 1967-68ஆம் ஆண்டிற்கான நிதிக் கோரிக்கை மீது வாக்கெடுப்பு

Date: 30 June 1967

Honourable Speaker of the Legislative Assembly, when the Leader of the Opposition spoke at the end, he said, “I did not support this cut motion.” [Interruption.] In substance, however, he spoke in support of it. Those who moved the cut motion formerly belonged to the ruling party. Because they were once in government, I accept that they have the experience and the right to explain how an administration should be conducted, how ministers should maintain relations among themselves, and how each minister should discharge the work of his department properly.

They possess experience and may offer us advice. Yet it would be a greater honour for them to hear me acknowledge their experience than for them to proclaim it themselves. From the very beginning, I have told those who were formerly in power: “If you offer suitable suggestions that will help us conduct our administration properly, we shall try to accept and follow them.”

If defects have indeed appeared in the conduct of this government, people concerned solely with the good administration of the country could have approached us privately and sought corrections. Instead, by raising these matters in the Assembly, they seek material in the morning for an evening claim before the public: “Do you know how wrongly this government is functioning and how thoroughly we exposed it?” Their speeches need be viewed only in that light, not as matters demanding serious attention.

They alleged that the members of this government do not know how to administer anything properly, that not even one minister knows which department belongs to him, and that ministers speak about matters unrelated to their respective portfolios.

There is no doubt that we are new to office. But while you acquired fifteen years of experience in government, we observed and understood its patterns even while we were out of power. That is why every minister, individually and collectively, has answered your allegations and shown that no such improper statements were made. If you say merely that their replies cannot be challenged under the formal rules of the Assembly, that will not give me complete satisfaction or happiness.

Let me state the truth. Newspaper reports cannot reproduce every speech in full. I am not accusing newspapers of reporting speeches incorrectly. As the Leader of the Opposition himself said, I am a man whom newspapers praised, nurtured, and helped to grow. That is true. More than that, I proudly say that I myself belong to the journalistic fraternity. Nevertheless, no newspaper can publish every minister’s speech in full; there is simply no room for it.

If, on reading an excerpt, you wondered whether something had happened in one way or another, anyone genuinely interested in good government could have asked us directly. But that was not your intention. Your intention was to cite numerous matters in the Assembly so that you could tell people outside that this Cabinet had committed many errors. Although I hold you in high regard, I state categorically that I attach no importance to that intention.

So long as collective responsibility exists in this Cabinet, I do not believe there is a rule that ministers must speak only about the departments assigned to them. They must, however, ensure that one minister’s statement does not contradict another’s. In that respect, the ministers have committed no error. Since you have now spoken at such length, I trust that your speech may help ensure that no such occurrence arises hereafter either.

Our friend Mr S. Poovaragan said that ministers, when travelling outside, should not dine at private homes. There is little time—

**Mr S. Poovaragan:** I did not speak about dining; I spoke only about staying there.

**Honourable C. N. Annadurai:** Staying and accepting feasts are both inappropriate. Ministers need a secure and proper arrangement, and to that extent I welcome the purpose of his suggestion. But when the matter is enlarged upon, other things occur to us as well. Printed menus still exist recording the private homes at which former ministers dined and the feasts of fifty-four dishes or thirty-two dishes served to them. Those records have not disappeared. When they enjoyed so much hospitality as ministers, we were only pleased; we did not ask, “Why did you eat there?”

When I was a member of this Assembly and Mr Kamaraj was Chief Minister, the two of us dined at a private home after conducting the Thandalam conference together. If it is thought that dining or staying in a private home will lead a minister astray, I wish to say that there are no such weak people among us.

Perhaps, drawing upon earlier experience in which accepting a meal led someone to grant favours, our friends offered their warning with the good intention that we should not travel the same mistaken path. I take it in that spirit.

I have great confidence that no one among us suffers from that weakness. Even so, I wish to tell my ministers clearly at this moment that, as far as possible, they should avoid staying or dining in private homes. I address that instruction to them and to myself.

Our friend may not know that our circulars to all district organisations already deal with invitations from private dignitaries who propose feasts during our tours. This is not a lesson we had to learn only after becoming ministers. There is no need to teach us even that elementary lesson. We know these matters and have been taking appropriate measures.

Many friends spoke in defence of government officers. I sincerely welcome that. If a country’s administration is to function properly, both the ruling party and the opposition must show affection and concern toward its officials. I therefore welcome their concern for the officers.

But there is no condition here in which every officer must end each day fearing, “Today has passed; what will happen to me tomorrow?” No officer faces such danger. You portray yourselves as rescuers granting *Gajendra Moksha*: as though the crocodile had seized Gajendra’s leg, he had cried “O Primal Lord!”, and Mahavishnu had rushed forth saying, “I have come!” You act as though someone cried, “Save us, save us,” and you answered, “We have come.” That performance is excessive.

Had you stopped within reasonable limits, this could have remained a genuine issue. We know the use to which you put it. If you speak so that newspapers supporting you may print headlines such as “So-and-so Roars for the Officers,” take as much headline space as the papers can provide. I shall not stand in your way.

Mr Vinayagam, or perhaps the Leader of the Opposition—I do not now recall which—said that senior officials had all been pushed down to the rank of tahsildar. What actually happened was this. During their administration, the one personal assistant attached to each District Collector became two. By the time we assumed office, every Collector had four personal assistants. After consulting Revenue Department officials, it was decided that four personal assistants were unnecessary and that two posts should be removed. When those officers ceased to be personal assistants, they returned to the posts properly belonging to them.

Friends also referred to the Police Department. We invited its head and asked where savings could be made. With his agreement—and, to tell the whole truth, upon his own proposals—certain posts were abolished or reduced in status. No one lost employment.

Similarly, some friends said that they did not know whether Panchayat Unions would continue to exist. Sleep peacefully tonight: the Panchayat Unions will remain. We intend no danger to them. This government wants Panchayat Unions to function properly; it does not intend to abolish them altogether.

As I have said about several other departments, when two or three persons perform the same work, this government seeks to reduce that duplication. As Mr Karuthiruman said, when a person holds a post, we must obtain the full benefit of that person’s work. You may have read Parkinson’s book. As in Parkinson’s Law, one task may acquire eight or nine officials, expanding until no one can identify who bears responsibility and each passes the matter to someone else. We have sought to bring such work under coherent control. We did not act from any delight in throwing people out, nor would doing so give me satisfaction. There is neither pleasure nor necessity in it.

Those employees therefore face no danger. Even if staff reductions become unavoidable, this government will take measures to provide the affected people with other opportunities immediately and continuously.

In the course of this debate, members exchanged many accusations. I too would enjoy replying to them. But it has grown late and the honourable members have been kept here for a long time, so I do not wish to speak further on them. My unwillingness to speak does not mean that I am unable to reply or that there is nothing to say.

Mr Karuthiruman said, “If any of our ministers committed an error, bring them to the marketplace.” I do not know which former minister has aroused his anger, or whom he hoped would be brought forward when he made that statement. Even if he is angry with ministers of his own former government, I bear no anger against any former minister, including Mr Poovaragan. No allegation has reached me that any of those ministers was involved in bribery.

Dr Hande referred to the burning of certain files, and Mr Vinayagam asked me to speak about it. That is a particularly weak point for them, and I therefore do not wish to say much. I have not examined every legal aspect. But as a question of justice, what the former Chief Minister did was wholly wrong, wholly unnecessary, and wholly unjustifiable.

The legal question may be argued from two sides. Indeed, I referred it to legal experts and have received two different opinions—not because I intend to commence proceedings, but because I wished to examine an interesting question a little further. Dr Hande demanded that the former Chief Minister be placed in the accused dock. Did so grave a crime truly occur? His words reveal his distress, but even he would not actually place that former Chief Minister in the dock.

The newspapers write that even Kairon and Patnaik could not be made to stand in such a dock; I do not know the details. The former Chief Minister did not make or do anything of that magnitude. I think he merely burned whatever came to hand. Perhaps, finding no other work when leaving office, he thought he might at least do this before departing. I do not consider that any enormous injustice occurred. In my view, even had those papers remained, they contained no great secret; what he burned was ordinary material.

A newspaper reported him as saying to a correspondent: “If these remained, those who come after me would pounce upon the police officers; that is why I burned them.” Let me relate something from my own experience. During the most intense phase of the anti-Hindi agitation, a senior police officer suddenly telephoned Navalar Nedunchezhiyan one day and made an alarming inquiry concerning Annadurai. The surviving OCR of the exact words is badly damaged. Nedunchezhiyan, startled, denied what was suggested and was asked to investigate. That officer is now under my administration. Not only does he remain there, but he has been retained without the slightest harm to his official position. I have not felt even a trace of disgust or anger toward him for what he did.

I mention this because we possessed from the outset the administrative tradition that you acquired only after ten or twenty difficult years. If the former Chief Minister believed that we would hunt officials once this government assumed office, he not only failed to understand us; he was examining his own instincts. Perhaps they themselves would have hunted the preceding officials upon entering office, and therefore feared which officers we might hunt after they left.

You and we have entered this Assembly for a limited period after obtaining the public’s consent and permission. Government officers, however, have been trained for their posts, appointed according to law, and granted legal protection. I regard it as one of the highest qualities of government to provide every officer with proper protection and ensure that no harm comes to them under my administration. I think the former Chief Minister burned the files because he did not understand this.

I also wish to make one request of Dr Hande. When a skin eruption is beginning to heal, it must be given time to close. If it is scratched repeatedly, it will become a large wound. In the same way, repeatedly raising this matter will increase bitterness. After all, his party colleagues Mr Karuthiruman and Mr Vinayagam may sooner or later wonder, “Why does he continue to speak and ask about this?” I therefore humbly request Dr Hande not to keep irritating them with further remarks.

They are very good men. I know Mr Bhaktavatsalam very well, and Mr Vinayagam knows him even better. When you repeatedly say, “Put him in the dock and let us see,” it is not you who comes to mind; it is the attitude of these colleagues that makes one wonder whether some secret lies behind their insistence. This government will not become involved in such a course.

I accept with due respect the good suggestions you have offered. I assure you that I shall properly accept and act upon proposals explaining what should be done and how it should be done. I therefore ask you not to press these cut motions and to permit this demand for grants to be passed.

---NOTES---
The title line contains the isolated OCR fragment “ட” before “1967-68”; it is treated as noise in the English title while the Tamil source remains unchanged.

The source is a Legislative Assembly reply dated 30 June 1967. “Demands for Grants” and “cut motion” are rendered in their standard parliamentary senses.

Several lines near the end of image 6 and beginning of image 7 are heavily damaged. Their recoverable sense concerns Parkinson’s Law, administrative duplication, consolidation of responsibility, and continued employment protection; no missing numerical or procedural detail has been invented.

The opening of image 9 is severely corrupted. It records a senior police officer’s telephone inquiry to Navalar Nedunchezhiyan during the anti-Hindi agitation, but the precise allegation concerning Annadurai cannot be established confidently from the OCR. The translation explicitly preserves that uncertainty.

The names “Kairon” and “Patnaik” follow the Tamil phonetic forms கெய்ரோன் and பட்நாயக்; precise referents should be checked against the Assembly record.
